    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Folding and fracturing are intimately related processes with complex interactions between the remote stress, local folding related stresses and pre-existing fractures all influencing new fracture formation. The development of tools for the prediction of fracture orientations would have important consequences for reservoir and aquifer assessment. Here, the relationship between current the current fold geometry and fractures of Raplee Monocline, UT are examined. The approach involves: 1) field based characterization of the fractures on and around the fold, 2) the development of accurate models of the fold’s geometry using GPS, LiDAR and geostatistical interpolation and 3) the analysis of the fold’s shape using the concepts of differential geometry. By combining the fracture data, fold shape models and geometric analysis, correlations between fold shape and fracture characteristics can be examined and eventually predicted.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
